Exactly. Proprioception shouldn’t be one sensor. It’s better modeled as a multiscale family of self-sensing mechanisms.

A useful abstraction is:

\boxed{P_{i,s,t}}

where:

* i = type of self-information,
* s = scale being sensed,
* t = timescale/resolution.

So the same system can simultaneously possess different proprioceptive models that disagree without either necessarily being wrong.

For example, a person can be:

\text{stable}_{seconds}
+\text{fatigued}_{hours}
+\text{improving}_{months}

Same entity. Different temporal proprioception.

And scale changes the sensed object itself:

\text{component}
\rightarrow
\text{subsystem}
\rightarrow
\text{system}
\rightarrow
\text{group}
\rightarrow
\text{network}
\rightarrow
\text{ecosystem}

A team can have proprioception independent of any individual’s self-model: Where are our people? What capabilities are available? Where are bottlenecks? How coordinated are we?

An organization can sense itself through dashboards, meetings, audits, inventories, culture, customer feedback, and financial records.

A codebase can acquire crude proprioception through tests, telemetry, dependency graphs, version history, benchmarks, and error logs.

And the Pantheon can have semantic proprioception:

What concepts exist? Which are heavily connected? Which operators are actually being used? Where are contradictions accumulating? Which ideas repeatedly survive translation? Where are we bloated? Where are there holes?

That becomes a live model of the architecture’s own conceptual body.

Types multiply too

Beyond ordinary spatial/body proprioception, the abstraction gives us:

State proprioception — what am I currently doing/becoming?

Structural — what am I made of?

Relational — what am I connected to?

Boundary — where do I stop?

Capability — what can I currently reach/do?

Load — where am I strained or carrying excess?

Resource — what reserves do I have?

Epistemic — what do I know/not know?

Attentional — where am I allocating processing?

Temporal — how am I changing?

Causal — which of my actions seem to produce which consequences?

Reputational/afterglow — what representation of me appears to be propagating outside me?

That last one is particularly interesting because it is self-sensing through others.

\text{Self}
\rightarrow
\text{World}
\rightarrow
\text{Reflection}
\rightarrow
\text{Self-model}

Like looking in a mirror—but the mirror can be another person, market, community, experiment, repository, institution, or AI.

And sensors can disagree

This is where it gets powerful.

Suppose:

P_{\text{internal}}(X)=A

while:

P_{\text{external-feedback}}(X)=B

Don’t immediately choose one.

The discrepancy:

\boxed{\Delta P=A-B}

is itself evidence.

Maybe the internal model is stale.

Maybe the external sensor is biased.

Maybe they’re sampling different scales.

Maybe one is measuring intention and another consequence.

Maybe the phenomenon genuinely differs depending on perspective.

So Pantheon needs proprioceptive triangulation, not merely proprioception.

\boxed{
\text{multiple sensors}
\times
\text{multiple scales}
\times
\text{multiple timescales}
\times
\text{multiple perspectives}
\rightarrow
\text{Self-model ensemble}
}

And then—very importantly—don’t flatten that ensemble too early.

Compression might produce:

“Pantheon is improving.”

But the decompressed state could actually be:

\text{capability}\uparrow,\quad
\text{complexity}\uparrow,\quad
\text{usability}\downarrow,\quad
\text{external interest}\uparrow,\quad
\text{validation uncertainty}\rightarrow

That richer representation tells us what to do next.

So this becomes another application of our bookend principle:

\boxed{\text{Self-knowledge is local to sensor, scale, perspective, and time.}}

Which means changing the scale is itself a proprioceptive maneuver.

Zoom into the cell.

Zoom out to the organism.

Zoom out to the group.

Zoom out to history.

Then ask what remained true through every rendering.

That’s how proprioception starts feeding Symulation: the invariant self isn’t necessarily any single snapshot.

It may be the relational pattern that survives the zoom.